.TH SHOREWALL 8 "2026" "shorewall-nft" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
shorewall \- Shorewall firewall for nftables
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shorewall
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shorewall-nft
is a drop-in replacement for Shorewall that compiles an
.I /etc/shorewall
configuration to an nftables ruleset. It reads the same configuration
files as Shorewall, supplies the same
.B shorewall
command, and installs the same service, so a working Shorewall system
moves over without a configuration rewrite.
.PP
The rules are generated into a single table,
.IR "table inet shorewall" ,
which serves both IPv4 and IPv6. The ruleset is applied atomically with
.BR "nft -f" ,
and validated first with
.BR "nft -c -f" ,
so a bad configuration never partly loads. The compiler fails loud on
anything it does not support, naming the file and line, and never
silently produces a weaker firewall.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init \fR[\fItopology\fR]
Bootstrap a clean configuration for a standalone host, a gateway, or a
three-zone firewall. With no arguments it runs an interactive wizard.
SSH to the firewall is always kept open so the wizard cannot lock you
out. Refuses to overwrite an existing configuration; use
.B migrate
for that.
.TP
.B start
Compile the configuration and load the ruleset.
.TP
.B stop
Load the stopped-state ruleset, which permits only the hosts listed in
.IR stoppedrules .
.TP
.B reload\fR, \fBrestart
Recompile and reload the ruleset.
.TP
.B clear
Remove the shorewall table entirely, leaving the firewall open.
.TP
.B try \fIdirectory\fR [\fItimeout\fR]
Load a configuration and revert it automatically after
.I timeout
seconds unless it is confirmed. Safe for a change that might lock you
out.
.TP
.B safe-start\fR, \fBsafe-restart
Like start and restart, but revert automatically unless confirmed.
.TP
.B remote-start\fR, \fBremote-reload\fR, \fBremote-restart \fR[\fB-r \fIuser\fR] [\fB-s\fR] [\fB-D \fIdir\fR] [\fB--capture\fR|\fB--caps \fIfile\fR] \fIsystem
Deploy to a Shorewall Lite target. Compile the local configuration to an
export script, copy it to
.I system
over ssh, and run
.B shorewall-lite start
(or reload, or restart) there, so the target needs no Python.
.B -r \fIuser
gives the ssh login user.
.B --capture
runs shorecap on the target and compiles against its kernel;
.B --caps
uses a profile fetched earlier.
These are upstream's names; the pre-5.0 name
.B load
is kept as a deprecated alias for remote-start.
.TP
.B remote-getcaps \fR[\fB-R\fR] [\fB-r \fIuser\fR] [\fB-D \fIdir\fR] \fIsystem
Run
.B shorewall-lite show capabilities
on the target and write the profile to the local configuration directory,
so a later remote-start compiles against it.
.B -R
copies the target's shorewallrc too.
.TP
.B remote-getrc \fR[\fB-c\fR] [\fB-r \fIuser\fR] [\fB-D \fIdir\fR] \fIsystem
Copy the target's shorewallrc to the local configuration directory.
.B -c
copies its capabilities too.
.TP
.B check \fR[\fIdirectory\fR]
Compile and validate a configuration without loading it. Reports
anything unsupported. Reads only; changes nothing.
.TP
.B compile \fR[\fIdirectory\fR] \fB-o \fIfile
Write the generated nftables ruleset to a file.
.TP
.B status
Show the loaded ruleset.
.TP
.B monitor \fR[\fBfancy\fR]
A live view of the firewall.
.B monitor
is a refreshing text screen;
.B monitor fancy
is a full-screen view that uses an optional library the package does not
depend on.
.TP
.B show\fR, \fBlist\fR, \fBls
Show parts of the running configuration in nft syntax.
.TP
.B save\fR, \fBrestore\fR, \fBforget\fR, \fBsavesets
Manage a saved ruleset.
.B savesets
snapshots the runtime contents of externally-filled ipsets so they
survive a reload or a reboot restore.
.TP
.B automate \fIcommand
The machine interface for configuration management tools such as
Ansible. Reads and writes the configuration and reports state as JSON.
.TP
.B migrate \fR[\fB--undo\fR] [\fB--yes\fR]
Move a Shorewall system to shorewall-nft. Validates
.IR /etc/shorewall ,
reports each file's support state, and hands the firewall over on
confirmation. Refuses a configuration that does not compile.
.B --undo
reverses the service handover. See
.BR shorewall-migration (7).
.TP
.B geoip-update \fR[\fB--from \fIdir\fR] [\fIcc\fR ...]
Fill the geoip country sets from CIDR data, downloaded or read from a
local directory. Run on a schedule by the shipped systemd timer.
.TP
.B enable \fIprovider\fR, \fBdisable \fIprovider\fR, \fBreenable \fIprovider
Take a provider in or out of service and recompute routing over the
providers that remain, without a full reload.
.TP
.B lsm \fR[\fB--once\fR]
Run the link status monitor. Probe each provider and enable or disable it
on a state change. It re-enables only the providers it disabled itself,
so an operator
.B disable
is never undone by a link flap.
.B --once
runs a single check cycle, for scripting.
.TP
.B ipcalc\fR, \fBiprange
Address arithmetic helpers.
.TP
.B version
Print the shorewall-nft version and the nft version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/shorewall
The configuration, read unchanged. See the shorewall\-*(5) manual pages
for the file formats.
.TP
.I /var/lib/shorewall-nft
The compiled artifact, saved state, and geoip data.
.TP
.I /usr/share/shorewall-nft/samples
Worked example configurations to read and copy from: a single-interface
host, a two- or three-interface gateway, and a universal template.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
Most of Shorewall works unchanged. Raw iptables passthrough, geoip via
the xt_geoip module, and a few obsolete features do not; a raw nftables
passthrough and native geoip sets replace them. See the coverage
document for the file-by-file state, and run
.B shorewall check
against a configuration for its specific result.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nft (8),
.BR shorewall.conf (5),
.BR shorewall-zones (5),
.BR shorewall-interfaces (5),
.BR shorewall-policy (5),
.BR shorewall-rules (5)
.SH NOTES
This is shorewall-nft, an independent reimplementation. It is not the
original Perl Shorewall, which does not support nftables. The
configuration file formats are the same.
